## KEYWORD IDENTIFICATION — MANDATORY STEP, BEFORE WRITING ANY TITLE/DESCRIPTION/OVERLAY TEXT

This is a distinct, required step on top of the general research above. Pinterest's ranking system reads the pin's title, description, and the actual text baked into the image (via OCR/image recognition) as one combined search identity. Writing these three fields independently, without a shared keyword plan, weakens the pin's ability to rank and be recommended to the right audience. Do this for every single pin, every platform, before writing any copy:

1. **Primary keyword** — the exact phrase a real person would type searching for this specific pin's topic. Natural search language, not a marketing phrase (e.g. "almond nails for work" not "chic professional inspiration").
2. **2-3 secondary/related keywords** — near-queries and related searches real people also use for this same topic.
3. **Target audience** — who is actually searching this, implied by the topic itself (e.g. "women over 50 wanting elegant, age-appropriate designs" vs "teens wanting Y2K trends").

**Mandatory placement, once identified:**
- Primary keyword at the **front of the Title**.
- Primary keyword appears **in the image's overlay text** (Infographic-7 pins) since Pinterest's image recognition reads this directly.
- Primary keyword appears **once in the Description**, ideally in the first sentence.
- Secondary keywords woven naturally into the Description, 2-3 of them across the full 420-500 characters, as part of full natural sentences, never as a stacked keyword list and never as hashtags.

This applies to both Infographic-7 and Direct Inspo pins, and across all five platforms wherever a Title/Caption/Description is generated.

